(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A line in space is fixed by a point on it and a direction, or by two points. In vector form every point of the line is r = a + λb, where a is the position vector of a known point, b is parallel to the line and λ runs over all reals. In Cartesian form the same line is written with the point's coordinates and the direction ratios a, b, c in a symmetric fraction form.","f":[{"x":"r = a + lambda*b","n":"Vector equation: through point a, parallel to vector b (vectors in bold in the book).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.195"},{"x":"(x - x_1)/a = (y - y_1)/b = (z - z_1)/c","n":"Cartesian (symmetric) form; a, b, c = direction ratios.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.195"},{"x":"r = a + lambda*(b - a)","n":"Through two points with position vectors a and b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.196"},{"x":"(x - x_1)/(x_2 - x_1) = (y - y_1)/(y_2 - y_1) = (z - z_1)/(z_2 - z_1)","n":"Cartesian form through two points.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.196"}],"w":["A line in space has no single Cartesian equation; it needs the pair of equations in the symmetric form."]},"6.2":{"s":"The perpendicular distance of a point P from a line r = a + λb comes from a right-angled triangle: the hypotenuse is AP, and one side is the projection of AP on the line's direction. Alternatively, write the foot of the perpendicular M in terms of λ, use the fact that PM is perpendicular to the line to find λ, then find PM.","f":[{"x":"d = sqrt(|alpha - a|^2 - ((alpha - a).b/|b|)^2)","n":"Distance of the point with position vector alpha from r = a + lambda b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.201"}]},"6.3":{"s":"Skew lines are lines in space that neither meet nor are parallel. The shortest distance between them is along the segment perpendicular to both, which lies along b₁ × b₂. Two lines meet exactly when this distance is zero. For parallel lines the distance is found from the cross product of the joining vector with the unit direction vector.","f":[{"x":"d = |(a_2 - a_1).(b_1 x b_2)|/|b_1 x b_2|","n":"Shortest distance between skew lines r = a_1 + lambda b_1 and r = a_2 + mu b_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.203"},{"x":"d = |(a_2 - a_1) x b|/|b|","n":"Distance between parallel lines r = a_1 + lambda b and r = a_2 + mu b (the book uses the unit vector of b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.205"}],"w":["If (a₂ − a₁)·(b₁ × b₂) = 0 the lines intersect (or are parallel); the skew-line formula then gives 0."]},"6.4":{"s":"A plane is fixed by a point and a normal vector, by a point and two vectors parallel to it, by three non-collinear points, or by its distance from the origin and its unit normal. All of these lead to the scalar product form r·n = d, whose Cartesian form is ax + by + cz = d with a, b, c the direction ratios of the normal. Planes through the line of intersection of two planes form a family.","f":[{"x":"r.n = a.n","n":"Plane through point a perpendicular to n; with a.n = d it is r.n = d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.209"},{"x":"r.n_hat = p","n":"Normal form: p = distance from the origin, n_hat = unit normal; Cartesian lx + my + nz = p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.213"},{"x":"r.(n_1 + lambda*n_2) = d_1 + lambda*d_2","n":"Planes through the line of intersection of r.n_1 = d_1 and r.n_2 = d_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.215"}],"w":["In the normal form p must be positive and n̂ a unit vector; divide by |n| first."]},"6.5":{"s":"The angle between two planes is the acute angle between their normals; they are perpendicular when the dot product of the normals is zero. The angle between a line and a plane is the complement of the angle between the line and the plane's normal, so its sine (not cosine) uses the dot product. A line is perpendicular to a plane when its direction is parallel to the normal.","f":[{"x":"cos(theta) = |n_1.n_2|/(|n_1|*|n_2|)","n":"Angle between planes r.n_1 = d_1 and r.n_2 = d_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.217"},{"x":"sin(theta) = |b.n|/(|b|*|n|)","n":"Angle between the line r = a + lambda b and the plane r.n = d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.217"}],"w":["Line and plane: SINE of the angle; two planes or two lines: COSINE."]},"6.6":{"s":"Two lines are coplanar when they lie in one plane. Parallel lines always are. Non-parallel lines are coplanar exactly when the shortest distance between them is zero, that is, when the scalar triple product of the joining vector and the two directions vanishes. The plane holding them then passes through a point of either line with normal b₁ × b₂.","f":[{"x":"(a_2 - a_1).(b_1 x b_2) = 0","n":"Condition for the lines r = a_1 + lambda b_1 and r = a_2 + mu b_2 to be coplanar.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.218"},{"x":"(r - a_1).(b_1 x b_2) = 0","n":"Equation of the plane containing them.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.218"}]},"6.7":{"s":"To find the distance of a point A from a plane, write the plane in normal form r·n̂ = p. The parallel plane through A is at distance a·n̂ from the origin, so the required distance is the difference of the two. In Cartesian form this gives the familiar formula with the absolute value of the plane's expression at the point, divided by the length of the normal.","f":[{"x":"d = |p - a.n_hat|","n":"Distance of the point a from the plane r.n_hat = p (plane in normal form).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.220"}],"w":["Convert the plane to normal form first; otherwise divide by |n|."]}};
